<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-V-vector_fields" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
 <title> Fields </title>


<introduction>
  
       <p>
        A field describes a physical quantity or mathematical function that exists throughout space. 
        Depending on what is assigned to each point, fields fall into two main categories: 
          </p>
           <image source = "field.jpeg" width="30%"/>
          <p>
            Scalar field: 
        Assigns a single number (a scalar) to each point in space. Examples include temperature <m>T(x, y, z)</m> 
        in a room, atmospheric pressure, or gravitational potential energy. 
                 </p>
                 <p>
                   Vector field: 
        Assigns both a magnitude and a direction (a vector) to each point in space. 
        Examples include a gravitational force field, an electric field, or fluid velocity vectors across a stream.
                 </p>
                
                 
                   <image source = "field_2.jpeg" width="30%"/>
                 <p>
                  The color and contour map above illustrates a 2D scalar field: each grid point has a single numerical value 
                  (elevation) shown by color and contour spacing, with tighter contours indicating steeper slopes.
                 </p>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-line_integ">
  <title>The Line Integrals</title>
  <p>
    A line integral (also called a path integral) sums up a field along a curve
        <m>C</m> parameterized by <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> for <m>a \le t \le b</m>.
        Instead of slicing an interval into segments <m>\Delta x</m>, you chop the curve
        <m>C</m> into tiny pieces of arc length <m>ds</m> or displacement
        <m>d\mathbf{r}</m>.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Scalar Line Integral: 
              Evaluates a scalar function <m>f(x, y, z)</m> along a curve
                            <m>C</m> and multiplies each piece by its arc length <m>ds</m>:
                            <md>
                                \int_{C} f \, ds = \int_{a}^{b} f(\mathbf{r}(t)) \, \Vert{}\mathbf{r}'(t)\Vert{}\, dt
                            </md>
              Physical meaning: If <m>f</m> represents linear mass density along a curved
                            wire, <m>\int_{C} f \, ds</m> gives the total mass of the wire.
                            If <m>f = 1</m>, the integral equals the arc length of <m>C</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Vector Line Integral: 
              Measures how much of a vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> points
                            along the direction of motion along curve <m>C</m> using the dot product
                            <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>:
                            <md>
                                \int_{C} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}= \int_{a}^{b} \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{r}(t)) \cdot \mathbf{r}'(t) \, dt
                            </md>
              Physical meaning: When <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is a force field,
                            <m>\int_{C} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> equals the work done moving a
                            particle along <m>C</m>.
                            If <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is perpendicular to the path everywhere, the dot product is
                            zero and no work is done along that curve.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        Beyond these two types, there are some other forms of line integrals also appear in multivariable calculus.
  </p>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <em>Scalar Line Integral (<m>\int_{C} f \, ds</m>)</em>
        Evaluate
                <m>\int_{C} (x + 2y) \, ds</m>, where <m>C</m> is the line segment from
                <m>(0, 0)</m> to <m>(2, 4)</m>. 
                  </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Parameterize the curve <m>C</m>: 
        A straight line
                from <m>(0, 0)</m> to <m>(2, 4)</m> for <m>0 \le t \le 1</m> is:
                <md>
                    \mathbf{r}(t) = \langle 2t, \, 4t \rangle, \quad 0 \le t \le 1
                </md>
                so <m>x(t) = 2t</m> and <m>y(t) = 4t</m>. 
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
                Compute the speed / arc-length factor
                <m>\Vert{}\mathbf{r}'(t)\Vert{}</m>:
                <md>
                    \mathbf{r}'(t) = \langle 2, \, 4 \rangle
                </md>
                <md>
                    \Vert{}\mathbf{r}'(t)\Vert{}= \sqrt{2^{2} + 4^{2}}= \sqrt{20}= 2\sqrt{5}
                </md>
                Thus <m>ds = 2\sqrt{5}\, dt</m>. 
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
               Substitute into the integral and evaluate:
                <md>
                    \int_{C} (x + 2y) \, ds = \int_{0}^{1} \big(2t + 2(4t)\big)(2\sqrt{5}) \, dt = \int_{0}^{1} 10t \cdot 2\sqrt{5}\, dt 
                </md>
                <md>
                   = 20\sqrt{5}\int_{0}^{1} t \, dt = 20\sqrt{5}\left[ \frac{t^{2}}{2}\right]_{0}^{1} = 20\sqrt{5}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) = 10\sqrt{5}
                </md>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>                
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <em>Vector Line Integral (<m>\int_{C} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>)</em>
        Evaluate the
                work <m>\int_{C} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> for the vector field
                <m>\mathbf{F}(x, y) = \langle y, \, -x \rangle</m> along the quarter-circle
                <m>C</m> of radius <m>3</m> from <m>(3, 0)</m> to <m>(0, 3)</m> in the first
                quadrant.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Parameterize the curve <m>C</m>: A circle of radius <m>3</m> traversed
                counterclockwise from angle <m>t = 0</m> to <m>t = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> is:
                <md>
                    \mathbf{r}(t) = \langle 3\cos t, \, 3\sin t \rangle, \quad 0 \le t \le \frac{\pi}{2}
                </md>
                so <m>x(t) = 3\cos t</m> and <m>y(t) = 3\sin t</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
               Differentiate
                <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> and evaluate <m>\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{r}(t))</m>:
                <md>
                    \mathbf{r}'(t) = \langle -3\sin t, \, 3\cos t \rangle
                </md>
                <md>
                    \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{r}(t)) = \langle y(t), \, -x(t) \rangle = \langle 3\sin t, \, -3\cos t \rangle
                </md>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
               Take the dot product and integrate:
                <md>
                    \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{r}(t)) \cdot \mathbf{r}'(t) = (3\sin t)(-3\sin t) + (-3\cos t)(3\cos t) \\
                    = -9(\sin^{2} t + \cos^{2} t) = -9
                </md>
                <md>
                    \int_{C} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}= \int_{0}^{\pi/2}(-9) \, dt = -9 \left[ t \right]_{0}^{\pi/2}= -\frac{9\pi}{2}
                </md>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>              
                Notice that the dot product is negative throughout the path because
                <m>\mathbf{F}(x, y) = \langle y, -x \rangle</m> circulates clockwise while the
                quarter-circle moves counterclockwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        At <m>(3, 0)</m> (start of the path):
                <m>\mathbf{F}(3, 0) = \langle 0, -3 \rangle</m>.
                The arrow points straight down.At <m>(0, 3)</m> (end of the path):
                <m>\mathbf{F}(0, 3) = \langle 3, 0 \rangle</m>.
                The arrow points straight right. 
                At <m>(3/\sqrt{2}, 3/\sqrt{2})</m> (midpoint):
                The field points down and right tangent to a clockwise circle.
      </p>
      <p>
        At <m>(3, 0)</m>, moving along the quarter-circle takes you upward
                (<m>\mathbf{r}'(0) = \langle 0, 3 \rangle</m>).At <m>(0, 3)</m>, moving along
                the quarter-circle takes you leftward
                (<m>\mathbf{r}'(\pi/2) = \langle -3, 0 \rangle</m>)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>


</subsection>


<exercise>
  <p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
           For a curve parameterized by <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle 2t, t^{2} \rangle</m> on
        <m>0 \le t \le 1</m>, what is the arc-length differential <m>ds</m> in terms of
        <m>dt</m>?
        </p>
       
          <hint> 
 <p>
  Differentiate each component of <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>, square both derivatives,
    sum them, and take the square root.
  </p>
            </hint>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Evaluate <m>\int_{C} 3 \, ds</m> where <m>C</m> is the straight line segment
                        from <m>(1, 2)</m> to <m>(4, 6)</m>.
          </p>
          <hint>
            <p>
                            Integrating a constant <m>k</m> with respect to <m>ds</m> equals <m>k</m>
                            times the geometric length of the curve.
                        </p>
            </hint>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Let <m>C</m> be the line segment from <m>(0, 0)</m> to <m>(2, 2)</m>,
                        parameterized by <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle 2t, 2t \rangle</m> for
                        <m>0 \le t \le 1</m>.
                        What is <m>\int_{C} (x + y) \, ds</m>?
          </p>
          <p>
            Hint: Express <m>x + y</m> in terms of <m>t</m>, find
                        <m>\Vert{}\mathbf{r}'(t)\Vert{}</m>, and integrate from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>\int_{C} f(x, y) \, ds = 12</m> along curve <m>C</m> from <m>A</m> to
                        <m>B</m>.
                        What is <m>\int_{-C}f(x, y) \, ds</m> along the same curve traversed in reverse
                        from <m>B</m> to <m>A</m>?
          </p>
          <p>
            Hint: Does the physical length of a tiny piece of wire change when you count from the other end?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A force field <m>\mathbf{F}(x, y) = \langle 2x, 3y \rangle</m> acts on a
                        particle moving along <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle t, t^{2} \rangle</m> from
                        <m>t = 0</m> to <m>t = 1</m>.
                        What is the integrand <m>\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{r}(t)) \cdot \mathbf{r}'(t)</m>?
          </p>
          <p>
            Hint: Substitute <m>x = t</m> and <m>y = t^{2}</m> into <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, find
                        <m>\mathbf{r}'(t)</m>, and compute the dot product.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Using the same field <m>\mathbf{F}(x, y) = \langle 2x, 3y \rangle</m> and path
                        <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle t, t^{2} \rangle</m> for <m>0 \le t \le 1</m> from
                        the previous question, what is the total work
                        <m>\int_{C} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>?
          </p>
          <p>
            Hint: Integrate <m>2t + 6t^{3}</m> term by term from <m>t = 0</m> to <m>t = 1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Let <m>\mathbf{F}(x, y) = \langle -y, x \rangle</m> and let <m>C</m> be the unit
                        circle <m>x^{2} + y^{2} = 1</m> traversed once counterclockwise from
                        <m>(1, 0)</m> back to <m>(1, 0)</m>.
                        What is the circulation <m>\oint_{C} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A vector field <m>\mathbf{F}= \nabla f</m> is conservative with potential
                        function <m>f(x, y) = x^{2} y + 3y</m>.
                        What is <m>\int_{C} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> along any smooth path
                        <m>C</m> from <m>(1, 2)</m> to <m>(3, 1)</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
     </p>
</exercise>
</section>